#: ../../../ecosystem.md:1
msgid "Ecosystem"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ecosystem.md:3
msgid ""
"PyGMT provides a Python interface to the Generic Mapping Tools (GMT), which "
"is a command line program that provides a wide range of tools for "
"manipulating geospatial data and making publication-quality maps and "
"figures. PyGMT integrates well with the [scientific Python ecosystem]"
"(https://scientific-python.org/), with [NumPy][] for its fundamental array "
"data structure, [pandas][] for tabular data I/O and [xarray][] for raster "
"grids/images/cubes I/O."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ecosystem.md:10
msgid ""
"In addition to these core dependencies, PyGMT also relies on several "
"optional packages to provide additional functionality for users."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ecosystem.md:13
msgid ""
"*This page was adapted from [GeoPandas's Ecosystem](https://geopandas.org/en/"
"latest/community/ecosystem.html) page.*"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ecosystem.md:15
msgid "PyGMT dependencies"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ecosystem.md:17
msgid ""
"_Asterisk (*) after the package name indicates the package is a required "
"dependency of PyGMT._"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ecosystem.md:19
msgid "NumPy*"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ecosystem.md:21
msgid ""
"[NumPy][] is the fundamental package for scientific computing in Python. It "
"is a Python library that provides a multidimensional array object, various "
"derived objects (such as masked arrays and matrices), and an assortment of "
"routines for fast operations on arrays, including mathematical, logical, "
"shape manipulation, sorting, selecting, I/O, discrete Fourier transforms, "
"basic linear algebra, basic statistical operations, random simulation and "
"much more."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ecosystem.md:28
msgid "pandas*"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ecosystem.md:30
msgid ""
"[pandas][] is a Python package providing fast, flexible, and expressive data "
"structures designed to make working with \"relational\" or \"labeled\" data "
"both easy and intuitive. It aims to be the fundamental high-level building "
"block for doing practical, real-world data analysis in Python."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ecosystem.md:35
msgid "xarray*"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ecosystem.md:37
msgid ""
"[xarray][] is an open source project and Python package that introduces "
"labels in the form of dimensions, coordinates, and attributes on top of raw "
"NumPy-like arrays, which allows for more intuitive, more concise, and less "
"error-prone user experience."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ecosystem.md:41
msgid "IPython"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ecosystem.md:43
msgid ""
"[IPython][] provides a rich toolkit to help you make the most of using "
"Python interactively. Its main components are a powerful interactive Python "
"shell and a Jupyter kernel to work with Python code in Jupyter notebooks and "
"other interactive frontends."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ecosystem.md:47
msgid ""
"PyGMT relies on IPython to provide a rich interactive experience in Jupyter "
"notebooks."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ecosystem.md:49
msgid "GeoPandas"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ecosystem.md:51
msgid ""
"[GeoPandas][] is an open source project to make working with geospatial data "
"in Python easier. GeoPandas extends the datatypes used by [pandas][] to "
"allow spatial operations on geometric types. Geometric operations are "
"performed by [Shapely][]. GeoPandas further depends on [pyogrio][] for file "
"access and [Matplotlib][] for plotting."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ecosystem.md:56
msgid ""
"PyGMT doesn't directly rely on GeoPandas, but provides support of "
"GeoPandas's two main data structure, {class}`geopandas.GeoDataFrame` and "
"{class}`geopandas.GeoSeries`, which can be directly used in data processing "
"and plotting functions/methods of PyGMT."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ecosystem.md:60
msgid "contextily"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ecosystem.md:62
msgid ""
"[contextily][] is a small Python package to retrieve tile maps from the "
"internet. It can add those tiles as basemap to matplotlib figures or write "
"tile maps to disk into geospatial raster files."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ecosystem.md:66
msgid ""
"In PyGMT, {func}`pygmt.datasets.load_tile_map` and {meth}`pygmt.Figure."
"tilemap` rely on it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ecosystem.md:68
msgid "rioxarray"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ecosystem.md:70
msgid ""
"[rioxarray][] is a geospatial [xarray][] extension powered by [rasterio][]. "
"Built on top of rasterio, it enables seamless reading, writing, and "
"manipulation of multi-dimensional arrays with geospatial attributes such as "
"coordinate reference systems (CRS) and spatial extent (bounds)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ecosystem.md:75
msgid "PyGMT relies on [rioxarray][] in several aspects:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ecosystem.md:77
msgid ""
"To save multi-band rasters to temporary files in GeoTIFF format, to support "
"processing and plotting 3-D {class}`xarray.DataArray` images."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ecosystem.md:79
msgid "To write CRS information to the {class}`xarray.DataArray` objects."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ecosystem.md:80
msgid ""
"To reproject raster tiles to the target CRS in {func}`pygmt.datasets."
"load_tile_map`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ecosystem.md:83
msgid ""
"We're working towards avoiding temporary files when processing/plotting "
"multi-band rasters in [PR #3468](https://github.com/GenericMappingTools/"
"pygmt/pull/3468)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ecosystem.md:87
msgid "PyArrow"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ecosystem.md:89
msgid ""
"[Apache Arrow][] is a development platform for in-memory analytics. It "
"contains a set of technologies that enable big data systems to process and "
"move data fast. It specifies a standardized language-independent columnar "
"memory format for flat and hierarchical data, organized for efficient "
"analytic operations on modern hardware. The Arrow Python bindings (also "
"named \"[PyArrow][]\") have first-class integration with NumPy, pandas, and "
"built-in Python objects. They are based on the C++ implementation of Arrow."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ecosystem.md:97
msgid ""
"If you have [PyArrow][] installed, PyGMT does have some initial support for "
"`pandas.Series` and `pandas.DataFrame` objects with Apache Arrow-backed "
"arrays. Specifically, only uint/int/float, date32/date64 and string types "
"are supported for now. Support for Duration types and GeoArrow geometry "
"types is still a work in progress. For more details, see [issue #2800]"
"(https://github.com/GenericMappingTools/pygmt/issues/2800)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ecosystem.md:105
msgid "PyGMT ecosystem"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ecosystem.md:107
msgid ""
"Various packages rely on PyGMT for geospatial data processing, analysis, and "
"visualization. Below is an incomplete list (in no particular order) of tools "
"which form the PyGMT-related ecosystem."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ecosystem.md:112
msgid ""
"If your package relies on PyGMT, please [let us know](https://github.com/"
"GenericMappingTools/pygmt/issues/new) or [add it by yourself](contributing."
"md)."
msgstr ""
